{
  "dom_id": "step-q39403",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The third and fourth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $-60$ and $-76,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence.",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms ${a}_{3}=-60$ and ${a}_{4}=-76$ are consecutive, we can subtract to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{4}-{a}_{3} \\\\  & =-76-(-60) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=-60,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ -60 & ={a}_{1}+2(-16) \\\\ -60 & ={a}_{1}-32 \\\\ -28 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-28+(n-1)(-16) \\\\  & =-28-16n+16 \\\\  & =-16n-12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
